WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.990
Film Courage:&nbsp; Let’s go back to the word
assumption and the filter that the director

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:12.350
sees this story from.&nbsp; How much does a director
have check their own world view? Mark W. Travis:&nbsp;

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.099
Their own worldview? Film Courage:&nbsp; Yes.&nbsp;
Or the filter that they see the world through

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:24.679
which has years of good things, bad things,
traumas, abandonment, happiness, praise?&nbsp;

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:30.779
How much do they really have to know themselves
to see 'am I projecting this worldview (which

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:35.650
may not be correct) onto every story?' Mark
W. Travis:&nbsp; Okay.&nbsp; A couple things.&nbsp; First

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.590
off how much do they know themselves? Film
Courage:&nbsp; Yes.&nbsp; And how important is this?

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:45.260
Mark W. Travis:&nbsp; First of all, really important
to know themselves!&nbsp; Really important to

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.789
know themselves and I’m sort of struggling
a little bit with how much do they have to

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:53.629
check themselves, but, let’s go back to
know themselves.&nbsp; They really have to know

00:00:53.629 --> 00:01:00.809
themselves a lot. &nbsp; Know who they are…I
think the more you know who you are…the

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:08.720
more you know about your assumptions, your
prejudices, your view of life and the world

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.671
because of your life experiences and how you
see things through different filters, the

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:20.250
more you know what the better director you
are going to be.&nbsp; The more able you may be

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:29.110
to get out of the way.&nbsp; Your job is to get
out of the way and not try to impose something

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:37.420
on a story or on characters. There is a process
on The Travis Technique™ which I don’t

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:44.550
know if you and I have talked about much at
all called Write Your Life.&nbsp; It’s a very

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:51.340
important part of the whole process which
is storytelling.&nbsp; And storytelling, not so

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.390
much writing, even though it’s called Write
Your Life, but it’s autobiographical storytelling.&nbsp;

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:02.380
Taking an event in your life and telling one
event in your life.&nbsp; Maybe it only takes

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:08.440
3, 4, 5 minutes to tell this event.&nbsp; The
question is, when you’re telling an event

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:16.090
in your life, can you tell it honestly?&nbsp;
If you’re telling something that happened

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:22.790
to you (this is what I love about this workshop
and this way of working) that event is already

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.730
written.&nbsp; There is nothing to write, it happened.&nbsp;
This happened, this happened, this happened,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:32.740
this is the event, that happened.&nbsp; Done!&nbsp;
So there is nothing to create.&nbsp; What your

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:40.200
challenge is, how do I tell this story?&nbsp;
How do I tell this story openly and honestly.&nbsp;

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.770
Second question is:&nbsp; Do you know yourself
well enough?&nbsp; You are the protagonist of

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:54.250
that event.&nbsp; How well do you know yourself?&nbsp;
And how well are you willing to reveal yourself?&nbsp;

00:02:54.250 --> 00:03:00.960
In all your flaws and all your courage and
all your trepidation, expectations.&nbsp; Whatever

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:08.319
was going on in that event.&nbsp; Do you really
understand it and can you reveal it in the

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:18.630
story knowing that event, that little 3-minute
event in your journey, in that 3-minute story

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:23.209
is the only thing that the story is about.&nbsp;
What happened around you, let’s say it was

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:27.180
a birthday party you went to or something
like that and some strange things happened.&nbsp;

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.680
All those things that happened around you
and impacted you, that is not the story.&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.511
The story is how you felt, how you reacted,
what you thought, what you were feeling, what

00:03:35.511 --> 00:03:39.410
you were desiring, what you were expecting.&nbsp;
That is the story.&nbsp; And that’s the story

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:45.080
of any character in a movie.&nbsp; So going through
Write Your Life, can you even&nbsp; do that?&nbsp;

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:50.459
How well do you even know yourself?&nbsp; And
the better you know yourself and can see how

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:55.810
you operate at any moment, the better you
are going to be able to see other characters,

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.490
the better you are going to be able to work
with other actors.&nbsp; So this whole thing of

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:04.550
knowing yourself is crucial.&nbsp; And this is
why we teach Write Your Life to so many writers,

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:15.110
directors, actors so that they can start from
a point of self-awareness and self-revelation

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:21.340
before going into writing the next script.&nbsp;
Before taking that next role, before directing

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.500
that next movie.&nbsp; If you know yourself.&nbsp;
If you say “I now understand how I operate

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.699
as a human being.”&nbsp; You will understand
every character you work with much better.&nbsp;

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:38.360
So that is crucial. And then getting back
to your question about imposing your own world

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:43.469
view on it.&nbsp; You will be less likely to do
that because you will be more likely to want

00:04:43.469 --> 00:04:51.830
to honor the view of the characters.&nbsp; And
there is one other aspect of this and that

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:58.370
is when you read a script and it impacts you…and
I’m assuming that you have this script that

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.939
you’ve read and you want to make it into
a movie and you’ve been so moved by it and

00:05:01.939 --> 00:05:09.520
you say “I want to make this.”&nbsp; My first
question to you the director would be “Great!&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.580
Why do you want to make this? (And all that)
But what do you want to say?&nbsp; What do you

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:21.050
want to do?”&nbsp; Best answer to that is “I
want to create this as a movie so that viewers

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:29.039
will have the reaction I had to it.”&nbsp; Not
“So I can push this message on them.”&nbsp;

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:32.759
I was so moved by this, I was so moved by
these characters.&nbsp; I was touched.&nbsp; I was

00:05:32.759 --> 00:05:37.889
brought to tears and laughter and I went on
an emotional journey after I was reading this.&nbsp;

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:42.689
I want to afford an audience (a viewer or
an audience) the same experience, knowing

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:48.439
that for each member of the audience, it will
be different.&nbsp; It won’t be exactly the

00:05:48.439 --> 00:05:49.439
same as mine. Question for the Viewers:&nbsp;
How important is self-awareness for a movie

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:49.440
director?

